WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.140
JOE: Many autistic people will show signs
of distress before having a meltdown, this

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.090
is sometimes called the “rumble stage”.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:11.113
They may start to exhibit signs
of anxiety like pacing or pacing,

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.990
rocking or becoming very still.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.040
At this stage, there may still
be a chance to prevent a meltdown

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.290
happening, by using distraction,
diversion and calming strategies

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.250
and removing potential triggers.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:27.220
BETH: It can help to identify the cause
of the potential meltdown as it is a

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:29.620
reaction to an overwhelming experience.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:34.010
Recording what is happening before,
during and after each meltdown may

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:37.970
show patterns in its occurrence like
particular times, particular places, or

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.120
when something particular has happened.

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:45.169
Once a clearer idea of what may be
triggering meltdowns is more evident, ways

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.410
to minimise the triggers can be organised.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.290
JOE: Every individual with
autism is different, but sensory

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:55.120
differences, changes in routine,
anxiety, and communication

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:56.930
difficulties are common triggers.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.839
A meltdown happens when an individual
becomes completely overwhelmed by

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.990
their current situation and temporarily
loses control of their behaviour.

00:01:06.849 --> 00:01:10.479
This can be expressed verbally
by shouting, screaming or crying.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.980
It can also be expressed physically
by kicking, lashing out and biting,

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.420
or it could be expressed in both ways.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:21.540
BETH: It is not bad or naughty
behaviour, like a temper tantrum, as the

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.550
individual is completely overwhelmed.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:27.660
Their disorder means that it is difficult
to express their feelings in any other

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.780
way and the result is a meltdown.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.810
JOE: Meltdowns are not the only
way however, that individuals

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.380
may express feeling overwhelmed.

00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:39.070
They may withdraw from situations
and refuse to interact.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.979
If someone is having a meltdown,
or not responding, they should be

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:46.479
given some time to recover from
information or sensory overload.

00:01:47.139 --> 00:01:50.199
If they are in a public place,
they should be asked calmly if they

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:52.420
are OK and given time to respond.

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:56.400
Others should be kept away so
as not to cause more distress.

00:01:57.139 --> 00:02:00.830
Sensory stimuli and information
overload should be reduced wherever

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.320
possible by taking them quietly
somewhere calmer and safer.

00:02:05.219 --> 00:02:07.640
Strategies such as deep
breathing could also be used.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:12.160
BETH: When they are in full meltdown
mode, they may lack the ability to be

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:13.890
reasoned with, redirected or distracted.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:19.060
However, this level of agitation
does not just happen, it can be

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.180
anticipated and de-escalated.

00:02:21.719 --> 00:02:25.210
It is important then to have a
plan for what to do in a crisis.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.799
Details of events, triggers or signs
that a crisis situation might develop,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:33.890
should be documented along with
tools and strategies for keeping the

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.159
individual and those around them safe.

00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:41.350
JOE: Intervention steps and de-escalation
procedures related to each increasing

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:45.690
level of agitation, should be noted
and lists of things to do and not to

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.700
do according to the fears and needs of
the individual should be clearly shown.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.250
Previous information from past episodes of
concern should be clearly indicated along

00:02:55.250 --> 00:03:00.080
with information and contact details of
family, professionals or support who need

00:03:00.119 --> 00:03:02.490
to be contacted in the event of a problem.

